With AdvFS you can use addvol/rmvol to migrate storage from one device 
to another on-line.  This comes in useful if you are moving your file 
system from say an internal disk to a SAN, or moving from a smaller 
device to a larger device, or moving from unmirrored storage to mirrored 
storage, or from a slower disk to faster storage, or from a failing disk 
to a replacement (assuming it is still readable :-)

Up to 256 volumes (each volume can be up to 1 terabytes in size) can be 
added to AdvFS allowing file systems that can reach 256 terabytes.  The 
disks/partitions/volumes/LUNs can any mix of physical disks, or 
partitions, or LVM volumes, JBOD disks, RAID, SAN devices.  And each 
disk/partition/volume/LUN can be of any reasonable size.

Individual files in the AdvFS file system can be up to 16 terabytes in 
size.

I think Tru64 v5.1b can support 2 terabytes per volume allowing an AdvFS 
domain to grow to 512 terabytes.

AdvFS allows you to create multiple file systems sharing the same 
storage domain, rather than use separate disks/partitions/volumes/luns 
per file system.  When using multiple file systems in the same AdvFS 
storage domain, each of those file systems can have system enforced 
limits on the amount of storage the file system can use so one of those 
file systems does not use all the available storage.

AdvFS allows applications to use Direct I/O (think Oracle) which gives 
the equivalent of 98% of raw disk performance using a file system (not 
for casual use :-)

AdvFS is the physical file system in a Tru64 UNIX TruCluster.

AdvFS directories use b-trees to allow directories to hold huge numbers 
of files and still give linear performance for file lookup, create, and 
delete (but "ls -l" will still take awhile to stat() each file in a huge 
directory).

AdvFS includes utilities to balance the amount of storage used on 
individual disks in a storage domain.

AdvFS includes a defragment utility.

Tru64 UNIX v5.1b includes vfast, a kernel based continuous defragment 
utility for hot files.  vfast can be throttled so that it will not 
overwhelm the disk bandwidth.

                                        Bob Harris


