ajjahagirdar@yahoo.com wrote:
> AdvFS & LSM friends or competitor?

AdvFS is a file system, just like any other Unix file system.  There are 
several advantages to AdvFS over many other file systems

- It is log-based.  I can't explain the details, but this makes it 
basically crash proof.  Forget running fsck.  Not needed (and doesn't 
work anyway).  Tested (personnally) and works great.

- It's File Domain and Fileset concept make it incredibly flexible. 
Think of a domain as the logical equivalent of a physical disk and a 
fileset as the logical equivalent of a partition (slice).  The big 
differences is you can have many, many filesets on one domain and you 
can "grow" them on the fly (which means you can over allocate the 
physical disk).

Now is where things start getting more confusing.

There is an optional ($$$) set of utilities that allow you to ...

- make volumes.  What is amazing is that you can do this on the fly ! 
Yep, add or **REMOVE** a disk from a volume, no sweat.  Just be patient. 
  Easiest way to migrate data to a larger drive.

- cloning.  ie a snap shot of a fileset that can do whatever with

- striping

AdvFS is, IMHO, the best feature of Tru64 and I can't believe that HP is 
